<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bacolor</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bacolor is a town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pampanga">Pampanga</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. During the British occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> in 1762–1764 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-de-anda-y-salazar">Simón de Anda y Salazar</a> made it the seat of the Spanish resistance and temporary capital of the colony. Much of the town was buried by volcanic mudflows from Mount Pinatubo in 1995.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bacolor.jpg" alt="Bacolor" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ralff Nestor Nacor · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bacolor_Church,_Pampanga,_Jun_2024_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bacolor lies 9 km from San Fernando, 26 km from Angeles and 75 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, and is divided into 21 barangays. Its name comes from the Kapampangan Bakúlud, meaning a high, level rocky place or plateau. Records show it as a proposed settlement as early as 1571, the year the Spanish founded Manila. The lahar of Mount Pinatubo raised the town to about 20 m above sea level.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bacolor formally became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pampanga">Pampanga</a> in 1755, though the historian Luciano Santiago holds that it already served as such before. From 6 October 1762 to 30 May 1764, while the British held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> and Cavite in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, Governor-General Simón de Anda y Salazar made it the seat of the government-in-exile and raised an army of local people to retake Manila. A decree of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III of Spain</a> of 9 November 1765 made it Villa de Bacólor, one of only three villas in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, with a coat of arms. Father Anselmo Jorge de Fajardo, called the father of Kapampangan literature, was born there.</p><p>The provincial seat moved to San Fernando on 15 August 1904. American civil government in the province was inaugurated on 13 February 1901 by Commissioner William H. Taft at the Escuela de Artes y Oficios de Bacólor, now Pampanga State University. Japanese forces occupied the town in 1942, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hukbalahap">Hukbalahap</a> fought them until 1945. On 7 April 1950, during the Hukbalahap Rebellion, pro-government paramilitaries killed 21 farmers in the barrio of Maliwalu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the eruption of Mount Pinatubo on 15 June 1991, lahar flows buried eighteen of the 21 barangays under about 20 feet of debris, killing hundreds of people. The San Guillermo Parish Church, half buried, is now entered through what were its upper windows, and the Sunken Shrine of Our Lady of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lourdes">Lourdes</a> at Cabetican is a place of pilgrimage. The town counted 59,361 people in the 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bacolor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In August 1899 the soldier and writer José Palma wrote the Spanish poem &quot;Filipinas&quot;, which was itself drawn from a Kapampangan poem, &quot;Labuad Mapalad&quot; (&quot;Blessed Land&quot;), written in September 1898 by Mariano Proceso Pabalan of <b>Bacolor</b>, Pampanga.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lupang-hinirang">Lupang Hinirang (Chosen Land)</a></li><li><blockquote>Dawsonne Drake of the East India Company governed as deputy, while Simón de Anda y Salazar organised Spanish resistance from <b>Bacolor</b> in Pampanga.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-occupation-of-manila">British occupation of Manila</a></li></ul></section>
